Chapter 1

Lunar Constants and the Impulse Advantage

All derivations rest on the fundamental gravitational parameters of the Moon. Let

\mu_L = 4.9048695 \times 10^{12} \ \text{m}^3\,\text{s}^{-2}, \quad R_L = 1.7374 \times 10^{6} \ \text{m},

from which the surface gravitational acceleration is

g_L = \frac{\mu_L}{R_L^{2}} = 1.624 \ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-2}.

The circular speed at zero altitude (first cosmic velocity) is

v_c = \sqrt{\frac{\mu_L}{R_L}} = 1.680 \ \text{km}\,\text{s}^{-1},

and the escape speed

v_{\text{esc}} = \sqrt{2}\,v_c = 2.376 \ \text{km}\,\text{s}^{-1}.

The specific kinetic energy to reach escape is

e_{\text{esc}} = \tfrac12 v_{\text{esc}}^{2} = \frac{\mu_L}{R_L} = 2.823 \times 10^{6} \ \text{J}\,\text{kg}^{-1},

which is 1/22.16 of the terrestrial escape energy. This ratio alone justifies a lunar electromagnetic accelerator.

Theorem 1.1 (Vacuum advantage).
In the absence of an atmosphere ( \rho \equiv 0 ), the drag power P_D = \frac12 \rho v^{3} C_D A  is identically zero; therefore no aerodynamic heating limits the track length or launch cadence.

Theorem 1.2 (Seismic quietness).
Lunar ground acceleration power spectral density S_a(f) \le 10^{-22} g^{2}\,\text{Hz}^{-1} at 1\ \text{Hz} is 10^{6} times smaller than the terrestrial microseism band. This permits track alignment stability at micrometre level.

The core design problem is posed as a constrained maximisation:

\begin{aligned}
\text{Maximise } \dot{m} \quad\text{(annual throughput)} \\
\text{s.t. } v_{\text{exit}} &\ge 2100 \ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1},\\
a_{\text{max}} &\le 20\,g_L,\\
\text{cadence} &\ge 24\ \text{day}^{-1},\\
\text{lifetime} &\ge 30\ \text{years}.
\end{aligned}

Every subsequent chapter derives the physical consequences of these constraints.

