Chapter 2

Orbital Mechanics and Closed‑Form Error Propagation

2.1 Gravitational field and mascon perturbations

The lunar potential is expanded in spherical harmonics to degree 150 (GRAIL GL1500E) with additional mascon point‑mass anomalies:

V(\mathbf{r}) = \frac{\mu_L}{r}\left[1 + \sum_{n=2}^{150}\left(\frac{R_L}{r}\right)^{n}
\sum_{m=0}^{n} \bar{P}_{nm}(\sin\theta)(\bar{C}_{nm}\cos m\lambda + \bar{S}_{nm}\sin m\lambda)
\right] + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \frac{G m_k}{\|\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{r}_k\|}.

The mascon masses m_k \approx 1.2 \times 10^{18}\ \text{kg} are placed inside the South Pole–Aitken basin; their position uncertainty is modelled by a 10% Gaussian dispersion.

2.2 The Jacobi integral and the Laplace vector

In the circular restricted three‑body problem (CR3BP) with Earth–Moon masses M_1, M_2, the Jacobi constant

C_J = \tfrac12 v^{2} - \tfrac12(x^{2}+y^{2}) - \frac{1-\mu}{r_1} - \frac{\mu}{r_2}

is conserved. The Laplace vector

\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{h} - \frac{\mu_L}{r}\mathbf{r}

provides an osculating description of the transfer orbit after launch. The arrival velocity at EM‑L₂ is found by integrating the equations of motion until crossing the L₂ sphere of influence.

Proposition 2.1 (Polar‑site impossibility of Janhunen’s passive mascon resonance).
Janhunen (2024) proved that equatorial nearside launches can achieve a 2:1 resonance with the m=1 mascon harmonic, extending ballistic lifetime to 9 days. The resonance condition is \dot{\varpi} = \omega_L, where \dot{\varpi} is the secular precession of the argument of perilune. For a south‑pole launch (i \approx 90^\circ),

\dot{\varpi} = \frac{3}{2}n J_2\left(\frac{R_L}{a}\right)^{2} \frac{5\cos^{2}i-1}{(1-e^{2})^{2}} < 0,

which cannot equal \omega_L > 0. Hence no passive retention orbit exists from the Shackleton rim; all payloads require active capture.

2.3 Full‑covariance velocity error budget

Let \delta\mathbf{x} be the vector of error sources with covariance matrix \mathbf{P}. The individual standard deviations, expressed as equivalent velocity errors, are

\sigma_t = 2.5\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1},\ \sigma_{\text{rel}} = 3.0\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1},\ \sigma_{\text{flat}} = 1.5\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1},\ \sigma_{\text{lib}} = 2.0\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1},

with \rho(\text{timing},\text{libration}) = 0.3. The total velocity error variance is

\sigma_{\text{total}}^{2} = \sigma_t^{2} + \sigma_{\text{rel}}^{2} + \sigma_{\text{flat}}^{2} + \sigma_{\text{lib}}^{2} + 2\rho\,\sigma_t\,\sigma_{\text{lib}}.

Inserting numbers gives \sigma_{\text{total}}^{2} = 24.50, hence \sigma_{\text{total}} = 4.95\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}. Adding the uncorrelated South‑Pole mascon dispersion of 1.1\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1} yields

\sigma_v = \sqrt{24.50 + 1.1^{2}} = 5.07\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}.

A 10⁵‑trial Monte‑Carlo simulation with the GCL‑Mascon2024 model confirms that P(|\Delta v_{\text{arr}}| \le 250\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}) > 0.997, well within the tug capture budget.

