Chapter 3

Exact Kinematics of the Variable‑Acceleration Track

3.1 Inconsistency of the original segment lengths

We prove that the previously published segment lengths (2000, 9695, 4179)\ \text{m} with a 5–20 g envelope cannot deliver a final speed of 2100\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}.

Theorem 3.1 (Energy integration for piecewise‑linear acceleration).
Let the acceleration profile be defined as

a(x) = 
\begin{cases}
a_0 + kx, & 0 \le x \le L_1, \\
a_{\text{max}}, & L_1 < x \le L_1+L_2, \\
a_{\text{max}} - k'(x - L_1 - L_2), & L_1+L_2 < x \le L,
\end{cases}

with a_0 = 5g_L, a_{\text{max}} = 20g_L, k = (a_{\text{max}}-a_0)/L_1, k' = (a_{\text{max}}-a_0)/L_3. The specific kinetic energy at the end is

e(L) = \int_0^{L} a(x)\,dx.

For the given lengths, a direct evaluation yields e(L) = 440\,220\ \text{J}\,\text{kg}^{-1} and v_f = \sqrt{2e(L)} \approx 938\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}. This contradicts the required 2100\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}.

3.2 Minimum track length for constant acceleration

To achieve v_f = 2100\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1} with a maximum acceleration a_{\text{max}} = 20g_L = 32.48\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-2}, the shortest track is obtained by using constant acceleration throughout:

L_{\text{min}} = \frac{v_f^{2}}{2a_{\text{max}}} = \frac{4\,410\,000}{2 \times 32.48} = 67.88\ \text{km}.

If a short initial 5 g ramp of length L_{\text{5g}} is added, the total length becomes

L = L_{\text{5g}} + \frac{v_f^{2} - 2a_0 L_{\text{5g}}}{2a_{\text{max}}}.

For a 100 m s⁻¹ ramp (L_{\text{5g}} = 616\ \text{m}), L \approx 72.2\ \text{km}. Therefore the baseline track is set to 67.9\ \text{km} with constant 20g_L acceleration. Payloads requiring lower acceleration are accommodated on a separate, longer track or at reduced throughput—a parametric optimisation is given in Chapter 8.

3.3 Piecewise‑constant profile with jerk‑limited transitions

Define three phases of constant acceleration:

a(t) = 
\begin{cases}
a_1, & 0 \le t \le t_1, \\
a_2, & t_1 < t \le t_2, \\
a_1, & t_2 < t \le t_f,
\end{cases}

with a_2 = 20g_L, a_1 = 5g_L (or 20g_L in the baseline). At each transition, a parabolic blend of duration \tau = (a_2-a_1)/\dot{a}_{\text{max}} (jerk limit \dot{a}_{\text{max}} = 100g_L\,\text{s}^{-1}) is introduced. The closed‑form velocity and position functions are:

v(t) = v_0 + a t,\qquad x(t) = x_0 + v_0 t + \tfrac12 a t^{2},

with the blended intervals treated as separate constant‑jerk segments. The total length and launch time are obtained by summing over all phases. For the constant‑20g_L baseline, t_f = v_f / a_2 = 64.6\ \text{s} and L = 67.88\ \text{km} as above.

