Chapter 4

Electromagnetic Launch System Theory

4.1 Linear synchronous motor (LSM) equations

The double‑sided LSM uses a stator with p pole pairs per 100 m segment (pole pitch \tau_p = 0.5\ \text{m}). The travelling magnetic field of the sled’s superconducting Halbach array induces a back‑EMF in the stator windings:

\mathcal{E} = k_e v \sin(\omega t + \phi), \quad k_e = 2p B_g l_{\text{active}}.

The electromagnetic thrust is

F_{\text{thrust}} = \frac{3}{2} k_t I_q,

with k_t = k_e (SI). Synchronism is maintained by vector current control.

4.2 Segment power and simultaneous‑activation reduction

Each 100 m segment, when active, delivers

P_{\text{seg}} = F_{\text{thrust}} \cdot v.

At the design point (m_t = 2000\ \text{kg}, a = 32.48\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-2}, v = 2100\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}), F_{\text{thrust}} = 64.96\ \text{kN} and P_{\text{seg}} \approx 136.4\ \text{MW}. Only three segments (the one containing the sled plus the two adjacent) are energised at any instant, giving an instantaneous total of \sim 410\ \text{MW}. If all 159 segments were energised continuously, the installed power would be 21.7\ \text{GW}; the active‑segment strategy reduces the required grid connection by

\eta_{\text{red}} = 1 - \frac{3}{159} = 0.9811 \ (>98\%).

4.3 Electrical loss budget

Copper loss: Aluminium windings at 30 K (RRR 500) have resistivity \rho \approx 2.65 \times 10^{-9}\ \Omega\,\text{m}. A segment’s total winding resistance is R \approx 1.06\ \text{m}\Omega, giving I^{2}R = 2.65\ \text{MW} at 50 kA. Energy lost per segment traversal: 2.65 \times 10^{6} \times (100/2100) = 126\ \text{kJ}; over 159 segments, total copper loss \sim 20\ \text{MJ} (0.45\% of launch energy).
Switching loss: SiC MOSFETs switching at 21 kHz dissipate \sim 14\ \text{MJ}.
Eddy‑current loss: negligible in the laminated iron core.
Overall segment efficiency exceeds 99.2\%, validating the high‑efficiency claim.

4.4 Inductrack passive levitation drag

The Halbach array produces a lift force

F_L = \frac{B_0^{2} A}{2\mu_0}\left(1 - e^{-2kh}\right).

At high speed, the magnetic Reynolds number R_m = \mu_0 \sigma v \delta \gg 1, and the lift‑to‑drag ratio asymptotes to

\frac{L}{D} \sim \frac{2}{\pi}\frac{\sigma v \mu_0}{k}.

With our parameters, L/D \approx 200, giving a drag force F_D = 40\ \text{N}—five orders of magnitude below the inertial force, thus entirely negligible.

