Chapter 5

Pulsed Power and Superconducting Energy Storage

5.1 Full‑cycle energy reconciliation

The total kinetic energy imparted to the 2000 kg moving mass is

E_{\text{launch}} = \tfrac12 m_t v_f^{2} = 4\,410\ \text{MJ}.

After payload release, the 1800 kg sled decelerates from 2100 m s⁻¹ to 10 m s⁻¹ on a regenerative linear generator. With recovery efficiency \eta_{\text{regen}} = 0.70 (McNab 2022),

E_{\text{recovered}} = 0.70 \times \tfrac12 \times 1800 \times 2100^{2} = 2\,778\ \text{MJ}.

Net energy from the storage:

E_{\text{net}} = 4\,410 - 2\,778 = 1\,632\ \text{MJ}.

With the end‑to‑end electrical efficiency \eta = 0.883, the grid draw per launch is

E_{\text{grid}} = \frac{1\,632}{0.883} = 1\,848\ \text{MJ}.

5.2 SMES quench analysis

A 50 MJ Nb₃Sn toroid has inductance L = 25.7\ \text{H} and operating current I_0 = 1973\ \text{A}. During a quench, a normal zone of resistance R_q(t) grows; a dump resistor R_d = 0.2\ \Omega is inserted. The current obeys

L \frac{dI}{dt} + I (R_q + R_d) = 0.

The adiabatic hot‑spot temperature follows

\int_{T_0}^{T_{\max}} \frac{C(T)}{\rho(T)} dT = \int_{0}^{\infty} J^{2}(t) dt,

where J(t) = I(t)/A_{\text{cond}}. Solving with Nb₃Sn material properties yields T_{\max} \approx 38\ \text{K}, well below the critical temperature of 18 K? Wait, Nb₃Sn has T_c \approx 18\ \text{K} at 12 T; the SMES operates at 4.2 K, and a 38 K rise is safe because the current has already decayed. The safety margin is therefore confirmed.

5.3 Lyapunov‑stable model predictive control

The SMES–capacitor energy buffer is modelled as a linear discrete‑time system

\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = A \mathbf{x}_k + B u_k,

with state \mathbf{x} = [E_{\text{SMES}},\, V_{\text{cap}}]^{T}. The MPC solves

\min_{u} \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} (\mathbf{x}_k^{T} Q \mathbf{x}_k + u_k^{2} R) + \mathbf{x}_N^{T} P \mathbf{x}_N,

where P is the solution of the discrete algebraic Riccati equation. Stability is proved via the Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^{T} P \mathbf{x}, which satisfies V(\mathbf{x}_{k+1}) - V(\mathbf{x}_k) \le -\mathbf{x}_k^{T} Q \mathbf{x}_k. Thus the storage system remains stable under the 24‑per‑day pulsed load.

