Chapter 6

Structural Dynamics and Foundation Engineering

6.1 Track as a multi‑span Euler–Bernoulli beam

The track of length 67.9 km is supported on 13 580 carbon‑fibre piers (spacing 5 m). The governing equation is

\rho A \frac{\partial^{2} w}{\partial t^{2}} + EI \frac{\partial^{4} w}{\partial x^{4}} = q(x,t).

Separation of variables gives eigenfrequencies

\omega_n = \beta_n^{2} \sqrt{\frac{EI}{\rho A}},

with \beta_n L = n\pi for simply‑supported spans. Using composite properties (E = 230\ \text{GPa}, \rho = 1800\ \text{kg}\,\text{m}^{-3}, I = 0.02\ \text{m}^{4}), the first bending mode is 0.48\ \text{Hz}, torsional mode 2.1\ \text{Hz}. The launch pulse repetition rate is \sim 0.016\ \text{Hz}; no resonant excitation occurs.

6.2 Regolith foundation settlement

The settlement under cyclic loading is described by a logarithmic accumulation law:

\delta_N = C \log\left(1 + \frac{N}{N_0}\right),

where C and N_0 are derived from lunar simulant triaxial tests. After 10^{6} load cycles (30‑year life), the permanent settlement per pier is 0.11\ \text{mm}, easily corrected by the active piezo alignment system on every 20th pier.

