Appendix B — Orbital Mechanics: Proofs and Derivations

B.1 Spherical Harmonic Expansion with Mascons

The lunar potential is

V(\mathbf{r}) = \frac{\mu_L}{r}\Bigg[1 + \sum_{n=2}^{150} \left(\frac{R_L}{r}\right)^{\!n}
\sum_{m=0}^{n} \bar{P}_{nm}(\sin\theta)\left(\bar{C}_{nm}\cos m\lambda + \bar{S}_{nm}\sin m\lambda\right)
\Bigg] + \sum_{k=1}^K \frac{G\,m_k}{\|\mathbf{r} - \mathbf{r}_k\|}.

The mascon positions \mathbf{r}_k are drawn from GCL‑Mascon2024; their masses m_k have a 10% Gaussian uncertainty. This potential is used in all trajectory propagations.

B.2 Proof of Janhunen’s Theorem for Polar Launch Sites

Lemma B.1 (Secular precession rate). For a near‑circular orbit of semi‑major axis a and inclination i,

\dot{\varpi} = \frac{3}{2}\,n\,J_2\left(\frac{R_L}{a}\right)^{\!2}\frac{5\cos^2 i - 1}{(1-e^2)^2},\qquad n = \sqrt{\frac{\mu_L}{a^3}}.

Theorem B.2 (No passive mascon resonance from polar sites). For a launch from i \approx 90^\circ, \cos i \approx 0, hence \dot{\varpi} < 0. The Janhunen resonance condition requires \dot{\varpi} = \omega_L > 0. Therefore no m=1 mascon resonance is attainable; passive ballistic retention does not exceed 38 hours.

Proof. Substituting i = 90^\circ into Lemma B.1 gives \dot{\varpi} = -\frac{3}{2} n J_2 (R_L/a)^2 (1-e^2)^{-2} < 0. Since \omega_L = 2.6617\times 10^{-6}\ \text{rad}\,\text{s}^{-1} > 0, equality is impossible. Numerical integration of the full CR3BP with GRAIL mascon model confirms the longest survival time from the Shackleton rim is 1.6\ \text{days} (38 h) before impact or escape. ∎

B.3 Full‑Covariance Error Propagation

Define the error vector \mathbf{e} = [e_t, e_{\text{rel}}, e_{\text{flat}}, e_{\text{lib}}]^\top. The covariance matrix is

\mathbf{\Sigma} = 
\begin{pmatrix}
\sigma_t^2 & 0 & 0 & \rho\sigma_t\sigma_{\text{lib}} \\
0 & \sigma_{\text{rel}}^2 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & \sigma_{\text{flat}}^2 & 0 \\
\rho\sigma_t\sigma_{\text{lib}} & 0 & 0 & \sigma_{\text{lib}}^2
\end{pmatrix}.

The total variance of the scalar velocity error is \mathbf{1}^\top \mathbf{\Sigma} \mathbf{1}, giving

\sigma_v^2 = \sigma_t^2 + \sigma_{\text{rel}}^2 + \sigma_{\text{flat}}^2 + \sigma_{\text{lib}}^2 + 2\rho\sigma_t\sigma_{\text{lib}}.

Inserting the numerical values (Section 2.3) yields \sigma_v = 4.95\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}. Adding the South Pole mascon dispersion (standard deviation 1.1\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}) as an independent term, the final velocity error is \sigma_{\text{total}} = \sqrt{4.95^2 + 1.1^2} = 5.07\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}.

Monte Carlo verification: 100 000 trajectories propagated with GMAT using the GL1500E + GCL‑Mascon2024 model and launch conditions randomised with the above covariance. The distribution of |\Delta\mathbf{v}_{\text{arr}}| at the L₂ target sphere has mean 187 m s⁻¹, standard deviation 5.1 m s⁻¹, and P(|\Delta\mathbf{v}_{\text{arr}}| \le 250) = 0.9982.

