Appendix C — Exact Kinematics: Closed‑Form Solutions

C.1 Piecewise Constant Acceleration with Jerk‑Limited Blends

The acceleration profile is defined by constant phases a_1, a_2, a_3 and transition times \tau set by the jerk limit J_{\max} = 100 g_L\ \text{s}^{-1}. The general solution for a constant‑jerk phase is

a(t) = a_0 + \dot{a} t,\quad v(t) = v_0 + a_0 t + \tfrac12 \dot{a} t^2,\quad x(t) = x_0 + v_0 t + \tfrac12 a_0 t^2 + \tfrac16 \dot{a} t^3.

For the baseline 20 g constant profile, \dot{a}=0 and a_2 = 32.48\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-2}. The total launch duration is

t_f = \frac{v_f}{a_2} = \frac{2100}{32.48} = 64.68\ \text{s}.

The required track length (zero initial velocity, zero jerk phases omitted for brevity) is

L = \frac{v_f^2}{2a_2} = 67\,881\ \text{m} \approx 67.9\ \text{km}.

C.2 General Variable‑g Profile Optimisation

To minimise track length subject to a_{\min} \le a(x) \le a_{\max} and jerk limits, we pose the functional

L = \int_0^{v_f} \frac{v}{a(v)}\,dv.

For a_{\min}=5g_L, a_{\max}=20g_L, the optimal control that minimises L is bang‑bang: use a_{\max} as much as possible, with a small initial ramp from rest to a_{\max}. The exact solution with a 5g start phase to speed v_1 and then constant 20g gives

L(v_1) = \frac{v_1^2}{2a_{\min}} + \frac{v_f^2 - v_1^2}{2a_{\max}}.

The derivative dL/dv_1 = v_1(1/a_{\min} - 1/a_{\max}) > 0, so L increases with v_1; the minimum is at v_1=0, i.e., no 5g ramp. Thus the absolute minimum length for v_f=2100\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1} is the pure 20g solution, 67.9 km. Any payload requiring lower acceleration must accept a longer track.

C.3 Proof of Original Segment Length Contradiction

Theorem C.1. The triple (L_1,L_2,L_3) = (2000,9695,4179) m with a linear ramp 5–20–5 g cannot reach 2100 m s⁻¹.

Proof. The energy integral yields e_{\text{total}} = 440\,220\ \text{J}\,\text{kg}^{-1} (Chapter 3). The required kinetic energy for 2100 m s⁻¹ is 2.205\times 10^6\ \text{J}\,\text{kg}^{-1}. Since e_{\text{total}} is only 19.9\% of the required value, no physical profile with those lengths and acceleration limits can achieve the target speed. ∎

