Appendix D — Electromagnetic Launch System: Derivations

D.1 LSM Thrust Equation

Using the Park transformation, the instantaneous thrust force produced by one segment with p pole pairs and phase currents I_d,I_q is

F = \frac{3}{2} p \frac{\pi}{\tau_p} \psi_m I_q,

where \psi_m is the PM flux linkage. By equating \psi_m = k_t / p, we obtain F = \frac{3}{2} k_t I_q. The back‑EMF constant k_e equals k_t in SI units. For the 100 m segment, k_t is computed from the magnetic design; we take k_t = 0.22\ \text{V}\,\text{s}\,\text{m}^{-1} (validated by COMSOL).

D.2 Instantaneous Power Reduction Proof

Let N_{\text{total}} be the number of segments and N_{\text{active}} the number energised at any instant. The total installed converter power rating if all segments were energised is P_{\text{inst}} = N_{\text{total}} P_{\text{seg}}. With sequential activation, the peak demand is N_{\text{active}} P_{\text{seg}}. Hence the reduction factor is

\eta_{\text{red}} = 1 - \frac{N_{\text{active}}}{N_{\text{total}}} = 1 - \frac{3}{159} = 0.9811.

D.3 Inductrack Drag Derivation

Consider a Halbach array of wavelength \lambda moving at velocity v over a conductive sheet of conductivity \sigma and thickness \delta. The magnetic Reynolds number is R_m = \mu_0 \sigma v \lambda / (2\pi) \gg 1 for v > 20\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}. The lift force per unit area is

F_L'' = \frac{B_0^2}{2\mu_0} \left[1 - e^{-2kh}\right],

with k = 2\pi/\lambda and h the gap. The drag force is

F_D'' = \frac{F_L''}{L/D}, \qquad \frac{L}{D} \approx \frac{2}{\pi} \frac{\sigma v \mu_0}{k}.

For our parameters (B_0=0.8\ \text{T}, \lambda=0.5\ \text{m}, h=0.05\ \text{m}, \sigma=3.5\times10^7\ \text{S/m}), F_L \approx 8\ \text{kN} and L/D \approx 200, yielding F_D \approx 40\ \text{N}.

