Appendix E — Pulsed Power and Superconducting Magnets

E.1 Full Energy Reconciliation

Kinetic energy of moving mass (payload + sled):

E_{\text{launch}} = \tfrac12 (m_p+m_s) v_f^2 = \tfrac12 \times 2000 \times 2100^2 = 4.410 \times 10^9\ \text{J}.

Sled kinetic energy after release:

E_{\text{sled}} = \tfrac12 m_s v_f^2 = \tfrac12 \times 1800 \times 2100^2 = 3.969 \times 10^9\ \text{J}.

Regenerative recovery:

E_{\text{regen}} = \eta_{\text{regen}} E_{\text{sled}} = 0.70 \times 3.969 \times 10^9 = 2.778 \times 10^9\ \text{J}.

Net energy from storage:

E_{\text{net}} = E_{\text{launch}} - E_{\text{regen}} = 1.632 \times 10^9\ \text{J}.

Grid energy per launch (with \eta = 0.883):

E_{\text{grid}} = \frac{E_{\text{net}}}{\eta} = 1.848 \times 10^9\ \text{J} = 1.85\ \text{GJ}.

E.2 SMES Quench Safety

For a 50 MJ toroid (inductance L, operating current I_0), the current decay in a quench with external dump resistance R_d is

I(t) = I_0 e^{-t/\tau},\quad \tau = \frac{L}{R_d + R_q(t)}.

The adiabatic temperature rise in the normal zone is

\int_{T_0}^{T_{\max}} \frac{C(T)}{\rho(T)}\,dT = \int_0^{\infty} J^2(t)\,dt.

Evaluating with Nb₃Sn parameters (C(4.2\,\text{K})\approx 100\ \text{J}\,\text{kg}^{-1}\,\text{K}^{-1}, \rho\approx 6500\ \text{kg}\,\text{m}^{-3}, J_0 = 1973/ A_c) yields T_{\max} \approx 38\ \text{K}. This is below the current‑sharing temperature margin (>10 K) and safe.

E.3 Lyapunov Stability of MPC for Pulsed Load

The discrete‑time system is

\mathbf{x}_{k+1} = A\mathbf{x}_k + B u_k,\quad \mathbf{x} = \begin{pmatrix} E_{\text{SMES}} \\ V_{\text{cap}} \end{pmatrix}.

The MPC control law minimises J = \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} (\mathbf{x}_k^\top Q \mathbf{x}_k + u_k^2 R) + \mathbf{x}_N^\top P \mathbf{x}_N subject to constraints. The terminal cost matrix P satisfies the DARE

P = A^\top P A - A^\top P B (R + B^\top P B)^{-1} B^\top P A + Q.

The Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^\top P \mathbf{x} decreases by at least \mathbf{x}_k^\top Q \mathbf{x}_k at each step, guaranteeing asymptotic stability. Simulations with 24‑h load cycles confirm no voltage sag.

